-- **set-chronicle.** One 1536 × 1024 landscape plate showing the Chronicle register, three studies feathering into one continuous warm ivory vellum ground with no rules, boxes, or borders between them: a page of an old manuscript chronicle on warm ivory vellum, with a ruled brown ink margin, faint ruled lines, and in its upper left corner an empty square frame for an illuminated initial painted in vermilion red and lapis blue with a touch of raised gold leaf and vine tendrils, the frame left empty, the page otherwise blank; a hand-drawn map of the island of Britain in iron-gall brown ink with soft watercolour washes of green for hills and pale blue for the sea, straight thin red lines for roads, a wall drawn across the north, a few small ships with square sails off the east coast, no words or letters anywhere on it; and an ink-and-watercolour museum study of a long medieval sword lying on vellum, delicately shaded. This plate replaces the oil register: it is pen, brown ink, and transparent watercolour wash on vellum, with fine hatching. No figures, no lettering, no letters inside the initial frame, no pseudo-writing.
